text:
Anya: Heads-up — a lot of users hitting 429s during initial onboarding with the sample dataset importer. They see bursty spikes and the JS sample app doesn't seem to respect Retry-After.
Sam: Which SDK + version? Are they on the new client or the legacy wrapper?
Anya: New JS client v0.9.2 in the quickstart. Repro steps: run importer with concurrency 20 against hosted with default quota. After a few hundred requests we start getting 429s and the client just retries quickly -> immediate 429s again.
Kevin: I dug in — js client uses exponential backoff but ignores explicit Retry-After header returned by gateway. It uses a fixed jittered base backoff and maxBackoffMs = 1000. Not enough for onboarding quotas.
Maria: Oof. That explains the cascading failures. Onboarding users have low per-minute quotas; when the sample app parallelizes pages it spikes.
Sam: Do we have a quick patch to respect Retry-After? If gateway sends Retry-After: 60 we should wait that long before retrying.
Kevin: Yep, submitted a tiny fix in PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/js-sdk/pull/482 (draft). Core change: parse Retry-After and treat as absolute/backoff override. Code snippet we used in tests: ```js
// parseRetryAfter tries seconds or HTTP-date
function parseRetryAfter(header) {
  if (!header) return null;
  const sec = parseInt(header, 10);
  if (!isNaN(sec)) return sec * 1000;
  const date = Date.parse(header);
  if (!isNaN(date)) return date - Date.now();
  return null;
}
// usage
const ra = parseRetryAfter(res.headers['retry-after']);
const delay = ra !== null ? Math.max(ra, 0) : defaultBackoffMs;
await sleep(delay);
```
Maria: Nice. Can we also add a client-side concurrency cap for the sample importer? We should show a recommended pattern instead of raw Promise.all over pages.
sdk-qa: Suggestion: add a p-limit example and a small concurrency knob in the quickstart. Eg: ```js
import pLimit from 'p-limit';
const limit = pLimit(4); // start conservative for onboarding
await Promise.all(pages.map(p => limit(() => callApi(p))));
```
Anya: Agreed. For the quickstart we should default to concurrency 2-4 and include a note about quotas + how to increase. Docs need to mention how to check current quota and the Retry-After semantics.
Sam: Also check behavior across Python & Go SDKs for parity. Do they honor Retry-After?
Kevin: Python client already reads Retry-After and respects it. Go client uses golang backoff lib but I think it also reads the header — I'll confirm and file a tiny parity bug if not.
Maria: Could we add an integration test that simulates 429 with Retry-After to avoid regressions? sdk-qa: I'll add an e2e test in the SDK monorepo that spins a tiny http mock returning 429+Retry-After and asserts client waits before next call.
Anya: Great. Also, small DX note: onboarding flow shows a 429 page with no hint to check SDK version. Let's add a troubleshooting blurb in the console UX + quickstart.
deploy-bot: deploy queued for js-sdk:patch-482 -> staging at 2026-03-22T14:05:00Z :rocket:
Sam: Plan of action: Kevin finalizes PR to respect Retry-After, sdk-qa adds e2e test, Maria updates quickstart concurrency example and docs, and product will add troubleshooting blurb in onboarding. Assign owners?
Anya: I'll own the quickstart text + sample app change. Kevin owns the JS patch. sdk-qa owns tests. I'll open a GH issue to track delivery and link it in the PR.
Kevin: I'll mark the PR ready for review after adding unit tests for parseRetryAfter edge cases.
Maria: I'll draft the console blurb this afternoon and put it behind an internal flag.
Anya: Thanks all — this should cut down the onboarding 429 rate and reduce support tickets. :thumbsup:
